WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.405
Eigenvectors need a square matrix

2
00:00:06.405 --> 00:00:10.013
and even then they can go missing.

3
00:00:10.013 --> 00:00:14.652
This matrix A has the eigenvalue two, twice

4
00:00:14.652 --> 00:00:18.260
and only one line of eigenvectors.

5
00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.850
Today we ask a question that

6
00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:22.770
works for every matrix.

7
00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.530
Take the unit circle

8
00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:26.770
every vector of length one.

9
00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:29.570
Push each of its points through A.

10
00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.990
A matrix keeps straight lines straight

11
00:00:33.062 --> 00:00:34.955
so the circle comes out as

12
00:00:35.028 --> 00:00:36.630
a smooth closed curve.

13
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:38.331
What shape is it

14
00:00:38.331 --> 00:00:41.909
and how long is it in its longest direction?

15
00:00:41.909 --> 00:00:43.260
Pause and guess.

16
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.430
Here is the answer: an ellipse.

17
00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.689
Its longest half-axis has length four

18
00:00:52.689 --> 00:00:55.112
its shortest has length one

19
00:00:55.112 --> 00:00:58.120
and the two axes are perpendicular.

20
00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.973
Which inputs land on those axes?

21
00:01:00.973 --> 00:01:03.085
Run the picture backwards.

22
00:01:03.085 --> 00:01:04.258
On the circle

23
00:01:04.258 --> 00:01:07.621
the long axis comes from v one, along one

24
00:01:07.621 --> 00:01:08.012
two.

25
00:01:08.012 --> 00:01:10.593
The short axis comes from v two

26
00:01:10.593 --> 00:01:12.470
at a right angle to it.

27
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.520
Now forward again.

28
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.071
A stretches v one by four

29
00:01:17.071 --> 00:01:20.661
onto the long axis, and v two by one

30
00:01:20.661 --> 00:01:22.645
onto the short axis.

31
00:01:22.645 --> 00:01:26.140
Perpendicular in, perpendicular out.

32
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.706
Keep a faint copy of this picture.

33
00:01:29.706 --> 00:01:32.955
A gets there in three simpler moves

34
00:01:33.043 --> 00:01:35.501
read from right to left in A

35
00:01:35.589 --> 00:01:37.960
equals U Sigma V transpose.

36
00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:41.865
First, V transpose: a rotation.

37
00:01:41.865 --> 00:01:45.534
It turns v one onto the x axis

38
00:01:45.534 --> 00:01:48.630
and v two onto the y axis.

39
00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:52.914
Then Sigma: a stretch along the axes

40
00:01:52.914 --> 00:01:57.061
by four across and by one up and down.

41
00:01:57.061 --> 00:02:01.420
Now the circle is an ellipse lying flat.

42
00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.307
Last, U: another rotation.

43
00:02:04.307 --> 00:02:08.211
It turns the axes onto u one and u two

44
00:02:08.211 --> 00:02:12.017
and we land exactly on the faint copy.

45
00:02:12.017 --> 00:02:13.090
That is A.

46
00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.321
In symbols: A V equals U Sigma

47
00:02:16.321 --> 00:02:18.273
and V is orthogonal

48
00:02:18.273 --> 00:02:21.341
so its inverse is its transpose.

49
00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:25.337
This works for every matrix, of any shape.

50
00:02:25.337 --> 00:02:28.033
The stretches, four and one

51
00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:30.356
are the singular values

52
00:02:30.356 --> 00:02:32.680
sigma one and sigma two.

53
00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:37.404
Where do v one and the four come from?

54
00:02:37.404 --> 00:02:40.348
Multiply A transpose by A.

55
00:02:40.348 --> 00:02:43.620
The U's in the middle cancel.

56
00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.713
What is left is a symmetric matrix

57
00:02:46.713 --> 00:02:50.162
diagonalized: its eigenvectors are the v's

58
00:02:50.162 --> 00:02:53.610
and its eigenvalues are the sigmas squared.

59
00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.205
See it on the picture.

60
00:02:56.205 --> 00:02:59.164
A sends v one to four u one.

61
00:02:59.164 --> 00:03:03.449
A transpose sends u one back along v one

62
00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:06.000
stretched by four again.

63
00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:10.317
Round trip: four times four is sixteen

64
00:03:10.317 --> 00:03:13.470
and v one never left its line.

65
00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:18.047
So v one is an eigenvector of A transpose A

66
00:03:18.047 --> 00:03:20.590
with eigenvalue sixteen.

67
00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:25.345
For our A, A transpose A has trace seventeen

68
00:03:25.446 --> 00:03:27.848
and determinant sixteen

69
00:03:27.848 --> 00:03:31.853
so its eigenvalues are sixteen and one.

70
00:03:31.853 --> 00:03:35.658
Take square roots: sigma one is four

71
00:03:35.658 --> 00:03:37.460
sigma two is one.

72
00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:41.308
Then each u comes from its v.

73
00:03:41.308 --> 00:03:45.590
u i equals A v i, divided by sigma i.

74
00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:49.760
That one rule also fixes every sign.

75
00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.723
Why not find U on its own

76
00:03:52.723 --> 00:03:54.555
from A A transpose?

77
00:03:54.555 --> 00:03:57.671
Its eigenvectors are the u's too

78
00:03:57.671 --> 00:04:00.420
but each one only up to sign.

79
00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:03.157
Take u two with the other sign

80
00:04:03.157 --> 00:04:04.928
and build the product.

81
00:04:04.928 --> 00:04:07.085
The ellipse looks the same

82
00:04:07.085 --> 00:04:09.626
but the grid comes out mirrored

83
00:04:09.626 --> 00:04:12.090
and v two lands on minus u two.

84
00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:16.684
U Sigma V transpose is now this matrix

85
00:04:16.684 --> 00:04:17.446
not A.

86
00:04:17.554 --> 00:04:20.601
Taking u from A v over sigma

87
00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:22.560
fixes every sign.

88
00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.153
A common belief: the singular values are

89
00:04:26.228 --> 00:04:28.940
just the eigenvalues, made positive.

90
00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:30.093
Test it.

91
00:04:30.093 --> 00:04:34.512
The eigenvectors of A lie along the x axis.

92
00:04:34.512 --> 00:04:35.416
Under A

93
00:04:35.416 --> 00:04:39.633
this one keeps its direction and doubles.

94
00:04:39.633 --> 00:04:43.650
But v one turns, and stretches by four.

95
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.154
So the eigenvalues are two and two

96
00:04:47.154 --> 00:04:50.939
but the singular values are four and one.

97
00:04:50.939 --> 00:04:54.184
An eigenvector keeps its direction.

98
00:04:54.274 --> 00:04:57.158
Sigma one is the largest stretch

99
00:04:57.248 --> 00:04:58.960
of any unit vector.

100
00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.468
They do agree in one way

101
00:05:01.468 --> 00:05:04.663
four times one equals two times two

102
00:05:04.663 --> 00:05:06.130
the determinant.

103
00:05:06.217 --> 00:05:08.461
And for a symmetric matrix

104
00:05:08.547 --> 00:05:11.310
the two lists match, up to sign.

105
00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:13.249
One more test

106
00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:16.452
a rotation by thirty degrees has

107
00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:19.170
no real eigenvalues at all.

108
00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:22.332
But it does have singular values.

109
00:05:22.332 --> 00:05:24.291
It keeps every length

110
00:05:24.291 --> 00:05:28.040
so the circle stays a circle: both are one.

111
00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:34.359
So, for every matrix, the same three moves.

112
00:05:34.359 --> 00:05:37.696
Rotate, stretch, rotate

113
00:05:37.696 --> 00:05:41.700
A equals U Sigma V transpose.

114
00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:44.841
The v's are the eigenvectors

115
00:05:44.941 --> 00:05:46.636
of A transpose A

116
00:05:46.735 --> 00:05:49.826
the sigmas are the square roots

117
00:05:49.925 --> 00:05:51.819
of its eigenvalues

118
00:05:51.819 --> 00:05:54.810
and each u is A v over sigma.

119
00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.230
And sigma one, the largest stretch

120
00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.160
is not an eigenvalue.

121
00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:01.874
In part two

122
00:06:01.874 --> 00:06:04.992
we split A into rank one pieces

123
00:06:04.992 --> 00:06:07.260
in order of importance.

124
00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:11.117
And we let the SVD find hidden tastes

125
00:06:11.211 --> 00:06:13.770
in a table of film ratings.
